package learning;

public class Student_Abstraction {
	
	//static variable
	static String schoolName  =  "ABC Public School"; 
	
	//static method
	static void showSchoolName() {			
		
		//print the school name
		System.out.print("School Name  : "  +schoolName);
	}
	
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		// TODO Auto-generated method stub

		//print a message to indicate static variable access
		System.out.println("Accessing static variable directly:");
		
		//accessing static variable using class name
		//no object is created
		System.out.println(Student_Abstraction.schoolName);
		
		//print a message before calling the static method 
		System.out.println("\nAccessing static method:");
		
		//calling static method using class name
        //no object is required
		Student_Abstraction.showSchoolName();
	}
}

1. Package declaration- 
Packages are used to organize classes and avoid name conflicts.
This line places the class inside the learning package.
2. Class declaration - 
Public class means the class can be accessed from anywhere.
A public class named Student_Abstraction is created.
3. Static variable -
Static variables:
- Belong to the class, not to any object.
- Are shared by all objects of the class.
Because it is static, it can be accessed without creating an object.
schoolName is a static variable.
4. Static method - 
Static methods:
-Can be called without creating an object.
-Can directly access static variables.
This method prints the value of the static variable schoolName.
showSchoolName() is a static method.
5. Main Method -
The main() method is the starting point of program execution.
It is static because Java calls it without creating an object.
6. Print Statement - 
Print a message to indicate static variable access.
7. New Line and Message -
\n moves the cursor to the next line.
Prints a message before calling the static method.
8. Accessing static methoid using class name -
Static method is called using: - ClassName.methodName()
No object required.


